Here's a Java routine to reverse an array of integers, handling negative numbers and `null` values, without using built-in reverse methods:

```java
public static void reverseArray(int[] arr) {
    if (arr == null) return;
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        int temp = arr[left];
        arr[left] = arr[right];
        arr[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
}
```

**Usage Example:**

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] arr = {1, -2, 3, 0, 5}; // Note: null is not allowed in int[]
    reverseArray(arr);
    for (int num : arr) {
        System.out.print(num + " ");
    }
}
```

**Note:** Java arrays cannot contain `null` values if they are of type `int[]`. Use an `Object[]` instead if `null` is required.

**Corrected Example with `Object[]`:**

```java
public static void reverseArray(Object[] arr) {
    if (arr == null) return;
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        Object temp = arr[left];
        arr[left] = arr[right];
        arr[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
}
```

**Usage:**

```java
Object[] arr = {1, -2, 3, null, 5};
reverseArray(arr);
for (Object obj : arr) {
    System.out.print(obj + " ");
}
```

